 pawn now white really  should not be thinking about  losing this pawn right here instead  should only be thinking about  getting more material involved into the  game that's the whole point of  the danish gambit is to get as much  material involved into the game so that  should include knight two f  three  and this is really where the trap comes  from now this was played many years ago  by henry bird he was playing against  lasker  and so we'll go over his specific  variation  in this trap but i'm going to go over  lots of different things that you could  encounter  if they fall for it completely and play  incorrectly  we'll show you that as well as if they  don't completely mess it up how you can  still capitalize and have a  very good game so if they play queen to  g5 many times they're looking to capture  this pawn here on  g2 now that would be a mistake here but  it's tough to see right away  how that is a mistake but after queen  takes on g2  then white's gonna play rook to g1 now  this queen you can see  doesn't have a lot of options can't take  the rook here  on g1 or the pawn here on h2 both of  those are protected by this knight here  on  f3 can't take the knight it's protected  by the queen here on g1  can't move along this g file it is being  attacked by this rook here so the only  safe square  is queen to h3 now if if black's really  looking at this and says  i don't just want to retreat right away  let's go ahead and get an  in-between move and attack my opponent  with the only other piece that i can get  involved into the action right now  and that is the bishop so bishop down to  b4  check but luckily for whites  they can just play king 2 e2 now they  don't want to play something like knight  2  c3 because that does stop the attack  from this dark square bishop here on b2  eyeing down this long dark square  diagonal but  it doesn't really matter if the king  comes here to e2 yes  you have lost the right to castle but  that doesn't matter you're not r