e queen  is to play  queen to h6 but then  white can take here on g8  after the queen takes it looks pretty  bad this is check right here so  if the queen does not take maybe king to  d8 well then all of a sudden just rook  down here to g3  and black has just nothing else left to  do here the root can't take because it's  just going to fall  to this rook taking it on g8 but then if  you say well  if it doesn't take then it's just going  to fall on the next move with bishop  takes here on h8  there's no strong attack that black has  all this material here on the queen side  of the board can't move anywhere  so that just looks pretty awkward but if  they do take let's say bishop takes on  g8  if the queen takes here on a g7 then the  queen is just going to fall so  none of those options are going to go  well for black so after the queen 2h6  which is really the only good move  uh that just doesn't lose the queen  automatically  black is still going to end up losing  so if we come back instead of king 2 e7  which you already know is going to lose  uh maybe instead they play king 2 d8  and this is when bird actually played  this which  the move we're going over is the danish  bird trap  uh he actually played the move bishop  takes here on  g7 so both the bishops up here just  cutting off  every single square that black could  really go to attacking  this rook right here on h8 and then  lasker played the move  knight to e7 just saying okay yeah i'm  gonna lose  this rook here uh there's nothing i can  do about that let's go and get my knight  involved  into the game uh but this loses because  knight  two g5 attacking the queen there's no  real good squares for the queen to go to  uh the queen came here to h4 and then  there is a smothered mate here with  knight to e6 uh being smothered by the  pawn and the pawn is being pinned down  by the queen here  on d1 so it can't really move anywhere  and it can't come here to e8 because it  is  being attacked by this bishop here o